WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.950
Let u be an odd integer greater than one.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:05.880
Our polynomial has Gaussian
integer coefficients,

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.300
and its product with its star equals
u squared in the cyclic group ring.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:14.580
We can substitute any u
squaredth root of unity for P,

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.790
because these inputs respect
P to the u squared equals one.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.340
When u is three, there are nine
allowed inputs on the unit circle.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.560
We want the distance of
each output from the origin.

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:31.400
Write f of P as the sum of
a sub j times P to the j.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:35.060
Star conjugates each coefficient
and reverses each power.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:39.300
The term a sub j times P to the j becomes

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.940
the conjugate of a sub j
times P to the negative j.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.840
Both changes matter when the
coefficients are Gaussian integers.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:51.040
Now substitute a root of unity, zeta.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:54.320
Its inverse equals its complex conjugate.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:58.580
So the starred term is the conjugate
of the whole evaluated term,

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.620
a sub j times zeta to the j.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.440
In the complex plane, this reflects
the term's arrow across the real axis.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.760
Add the terms: f star at zeta
equals the conjugate of f at zeta.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.520
Polynomial star and complex conjugation
agree after this substitution.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.760
Evaluate the norm identity.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.820
The output times its complex
conjugate equals u squared.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:27.820
This is its squared absolute
value, so its absolute value is u.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:32.100
Every output is nonzero and
lies on the circle of radius u.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:37.180
Its angle may change with the input; its
distance from the origin stays fixed.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.860
For a concrete example,

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.940
let u equal three and
f of P equal three i P.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.520
Its star is negative three i P
inverse, so their product is nine.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.800
This is a Gaussian polynomial
example, not a Hadamard sign row.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.280
Send the nine input roots
through this polynomial.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:01.300
Multiplication by three i turns each
input counterclockwise by ninety degrees

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:06.560
and scales its magnitude by three. The
outputs lie on the radius three circle.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:10.160
The input roots and output
values occupy different planes.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:16.420
Compare f of one with f of omega, where
omega is e to the two pi i over three.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.320
Divide the numerator by the denominator.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:23.100
Their magnitudes cancel, leaving
a point on a separate unit circle.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:27.250
The phase is the numerator's
angle minus the denominator's:

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.640
the rotation from f of omega to f of one.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:34.980
Here it is clockwise one hundred
twenty degrees, giving omega squared.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:40.140
Equal magnitudes alone do not limit
this rotation to finitely many angles.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.020
We study that extra
arithmetic restriction next.
